      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14222207/assets/3f162b6b0c51a94c3c752f8a6ee59d4232c5bbb2fe619c7c6273f37805c281b0.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. What are the coordinates of the point $P?$",
      "worked_solution": "Any point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx = \\cos\\theta, \\qquad y = \\sin\\theta.\n$$\n\nWe're given that $\\theta=45^\\circ$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡{45}^{∘}=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}, \\\\ y & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡{45}^{∘}=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the coordinates of $P$ are $\\left(\\dfrac{\\sqrt 2}{2},\\dfrac{\\sqrt 2}{2}\\right).$",
